namespace gismo {
/**

\page as_parameterization_example as_parameterization_example.cpp

This example constructs an analysis-suitable parameterization of a planar
domain or of a volume from its boundary (gsBarrierPatch). The method is set
by the option \c ParamMethod; this page describes the default, the
PDE-based method, and its options (group \c PDE of
gsBarrierCore::defaultOptions, see gsBarrierCore::defaultPDEOptions).

\section as_parameterization_pde PDE-based method

The interior control points are those of a discrete harmonic map from the
parameter domain onto the computational domain, i.e. of a map whose
inverse is harmonic (elliptic grid generation). The method has three
stages:

1. \b H2: the elliptic grid generation equations in non-divergence form,
   L(x) = tr(adj(g) hess(x)) / tr(g) = 0 with the metric g = J^T J,
   discretized by test functions in the parameter domain (Anderson
   acceleration with the Jacobian as preconditioner).
2. \b Untangling, only if the H2 result is folded (det(J) <= 0 somewhere):
   minimization of Garanzha's regularized Winslow energy
   int |J|^2 / chi(det J, eps)^(2/d) dxi with
   chi(D, eps) = (D + sqrt(D^2 + eps^2)) / 2, a barrier against folds once
   eps is small, by Gauss-Newton or projected Newton steps with Armijo line
   search. eps shrinks with the folding; once the map is unfolded, no step
   may fold it again.
3. \b Regularized H1: the H1 discretization of the same equations (Laplace's
   equation for the inverse map, int grad(phi) . g^{-1} e_c dxi = 0) with
   det(J) replaced by chi; every step is shortened so that min det(J)
   decreases by at most a fixed fraction.

Without the regularization (\c PDE.Garanzha = false) the method is the
original one: H2 and H1 without untangling for planar domains, the H2 stage
only for volumes. det(J) is checked at the nodes of a Gauss-Lobatto rule
(which includes the element boundaries), and only where it depends on the
free control points.

\section as_parameterization_solvers Linear solvers and preconditioners

Small systems are solved directly (sparse LU, sparse LDL^T for the energy),
larger ones (above \c PDE.DirectSolverLimit unknowns) iteratively
(\c PDE.LinearSolver): GMRES for the H2 and H1 stages, falling back to
sparse LU if it needs more than \c PDE.InnerMaxIterations iterations, and
CG for the energy minimization. Preconditioners (\c PDE.Preconditioner):

- block Gauss-Seidel by coordinate component (preAApp::BlockGaussSeidel),
  with sparse LU or algebraic multigrid blocks: suits the H2 equations,
  which are those of the physical coordinates;
- nodal algebraic multigrid (preAApp::NodalAMG): the unknowns of one control
  point form a node. The H1 equations are those of the parametric
  coordinates while the unknowns are physical coordinates, so coordinate
  blocks are a poor approximation of the H1 Jacobian; nodal blocks are,
  moreover, invariant under rotations of the physical coordinates.

The automatic choice uses the multigrid block Gauss-Seidel method for H2,
NodalAMG for H1 and for the CG of the energy minimization. For thin domains
(few elements across), sparse LU is cheap and the smoothers converge
slowly; \c PDE.LinearSolver = 1 (direct) can be faster there.

\section as_parameterization_free Free interfaces

With free interfaces (gsBarrierPatch(mp, false), option \c --free), the H2
and H1 equations of every patch are written in its own parametric
coordinates and summed at the interfaces. This is only consistent if
adjacent patches agree in parametric scale and orientation. By default
(\c PDE.FreeInterfaceEnergy), the parameter domains are therefore rescaled
by chord lengths (gsBarrierCore::chordScaled) and the regularized Winslow
energy, which does not depend on the parametric coordinates, is minimized
to convergence (\c PDE.EnergyTol) instead of the H2 and H1 stages; the
result is returned on the original knot vectors. Patches may be oriented
differently: det(J) is taken with the orientation of each patch.

\section as_parameterization_energy Energy and Hessian

\c PDE.EnergyForm selects the energy of the untangling and of the final
energy stage: |J|^2 / chi^(2/d) (default) or the inverse Dirichlet energy
|cof J|^2 / chi, whose continuous minimizer is the inverse harmonic map;
both coincide in 2D. \c PDE.EnergyHessian selects Gauss-Newton, exact or
projected Newton steps (the exact Hessian projected onto the positive
semidefinite matrices at every quadrature node); automatically, projected
Newton is used for the final energy stage of volumes, Gauss-Newton
otherwise. A step without descent falls back to Gauss-Newton.

\section as_parameterization_safeguards Safeguards and fall-backs

- \c PDE.H1Safeguard: if the H1 residual norm exceeds this factor times the
  smallest one so far, the H1 iteration returns to that iterate and
  restarts with a damped step. Diverging runs exceed the default factor by
  far, converging ones rarely reach it.
- \c PDE.EnergyFallback: if the H1 stage does not converge, the energy is
  minimized to convergence from its starting point (which is only unfolded
  at the quadrature nodes) instead of returning that point.

\section as_parameterization_experimental Experimental options

- \c PDE.SkipH2: untangling and H1 start from the given interior (e.g. a
  Coons patch) instead of the H2 result.
- \c PDE.H1Form = 1: the weak form int grad_x(phi) . grad_x(xi_c) dx of
  Laplace's equation for the inverse map (det(J) replaced by chi) instead of
  the original H1 form.
- \c PDE.ExtraNodes, \c PDE.AdaptiveRounds: more Gauss-Lobatto nodes in all
  elements, or adaptively in the elements where det(J) <= 0 at the nodes of
  a denser check rule (\c PDE.CheckExtraNodes). This helps if det(J) can be
  positive up to the boundary, and harms where the boundary forces
  det(J) <= 0 (e.g. at the edges of a volume whose boundary surfaces meet
  tangentially).

\section as_parameterization_exampleAnnotated Annotated source file

Here is the full file \c examples/as_parameterization_example.cpp. Clicking
on a function or class name will lead you to its reference documentation.

\include as_parameterization_example.cpp

*/
}
